----------------------- REVIEW 1 ---------------------
SUBMISSION: 8
TITLE: Calculating Sufficient Reasons for Random Forest Classifiers
AUTHORS: Markus Iser

----------- Overall evaluation -----------
SCORE: 2 (accept)
----- TEXT:
The paper investigates the use of a SAT solver to explain predictions from random forests (boosted trees/tree ensembles).

The paper reads well and it will be an interesting contribution to the workshop. The author should consult a recent publication on related topic [1] (accepted to AAAI22 with only a preprint available; disclaimer: I'm not the author). Their results seem to be scaling better than the presented paper, even though this paper is not trying to enumerate all prime implications, which eventually is bound to blow up.

The author should try to spell out early what kind of explanations are to be shown. Prime implications on what kind of variables? Ideally there would be a small example.

Comments
--------

The set of classes K should be finite.

Presumably all the encoding is using a 1-hot representation?

I'm confused about what beta does.  The text says that is a single variable but I would expect that the formula needs to decide whether there is a (labeled) edge n->m for any n<m.

I'm unclear on the concept of "explanation clause".  Why is it separate from the rest of the encoding? Why do we need this term?

I'm having trouble understanding Algorithm 1. Let F={x or y}, with model x=y=1, minim is set to {-x,-y}, F union minim is unsat, so P is now {x, y}, which isn't right. It seems that there are some double curly braces missing somewhere but I'm not sure if that's enough to fix it.

My understanding is that Alg1 should be a standard monotone predicate minimization (e.g. [2]), the author should refer which existing algorithm he's using. 

Minor
-----

"devise a prediction function which generalizes to yet unseen..."  which -> that

The symbol f seems to be used in multiple roles, which is confusing.

"As thresholds are chosen independently the size"  insert comma after "independently".

"Recapitulate how classification works in the random forests described in" I would assume this sentence is not supposed to be in the imperative mode.

References
----------
